{
  "id": "generate-parentheses-count",
  "questionId": "generate-parentheses-count",
  "slug": "generate-parentheses-count",
  "title": "Count of Valid Parentheses Combinations",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Backtracking",
  "patterns": [
    "backtracking"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you never need to build the full string first and check it - can you prune invalid strings while building them?",
    "track how many '(' and how many ')' you've placed so far as you recurse",
    "you can place '(' whenever you've used fewer than n of them, and you can place ')' whenever you've used fewer close than open so far",
    "count a complete valid string (add 1 to your running total) once you've placed 2n characters total - you don't need to store the strings themselves, just count the leaves of the recursion tree"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "allowing a ')' to be placed even when it would exceed the number of '(' placed so far, producing invalid prefixes",
    "forgetting the base case, so recursion never terminates or over/under counts",
    "trying to enumerate and store every string just to count them, which is unnecessary extra work"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an integer `n`, the number of pairs of parentheses.\n\n**Count how many distinct strings of length 2n, made only of `(` and `)` characters, are well-formed.**\n\nA string is well-formed if every prefix has at least as many `(` as `)`, and the total counts of `(` and `)` are equal.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 12",
      "the result fits within a 32-bit signed integer"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (only the empty string, answer is 1)",
      "n = 1 (smallest nontrivial case)",
      "a moderately large n to check the count grows correctly (Catalan numbers)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
